snapshot[23726-35626 of 796724]:
    @.922 text ". The Moon was volcanically active until 1.2 billion years ago, surfacing lava mostly on the thinner near side of the Moon, filling ancient craters, which through cooling formed the today prominently visible dark plains of "
    @.923 link "basalt" description="Basalt" url="/wiki/Basalt"
    @.925 text " called "
    @.926 link "maria" description="Lunar mare" url="/wiki/Lunar_mare"
    @.928 text " ('seas'). The "
    @.929 link "origin of the Moon" description="Origin of the Moon" url="/wiki/Origin_of_the_Moon"
    @.931 text " is not clear, although it is thought to have been formed out of material from Earth that was ejected by "
    @.932 link "a giant impact" description="Giant-impact hypothesis" url="/wiki/Giant-impact_hypothesis"
    @.934 text " of a "
    @.935 link "Mars-sized body" description="Theia (planet)" url="/wiki/Theia_(planet)"
    @.937 text " 4.51billion years ago, not long after "
    @.940 link "Earth's formation" description="Age of Earth" url="/wiki/Age_of_Earth"
    @.942 text ".From a distance, the day and night phases of the lunar day are visible as the "
    @.944 link "lunar phases" description="Lunar phases" url="/wiki/Lunar_phases"
    @.946 text ", and when the Moon passes through "
    @.947 link "Earth's shadow" url="/wiki/Earth's_shadow"
    @.949 text " a "
    @.950 link "lunar eclipse" description="Lunar eclipse" url="/wiki/Lunar_eclipse"
    @.952 text " is observable. The Moon's "
    @.953 link "apparent size" description="Apparent size" url="/wiki/Apparent_size"
    @.955 text " in Earth's sky is about the same as that of the Sun, which causes it to cover the Sun completely during a total "
    @.956 link "solar eclipse" description="Solar eclipse" url="/wiki/Solar_eclipse"
    @.958 text ". The Moon is the "
    @.959 link "brightest celestial object" description="List of brightest natural objects in the sky" url="/wiki/List_of_brightest_natural_objects_in_the_sky"
    @.961 text " in Earth's "
    @.962 link "night sky" description="Night sky" url="/wiki/Night_sky"
    @.964 text " because of its large apparent size, while the "
    @.965 link "reflectance" description="Reflectance" url="/wiki/Reflectance"
    @.967 text " (albedo) of its surface is comparable to that of "
    @.968 link "asphalt" description="Asphalt concrete" url="/wiki/Asphalt_concrete"
    @.970 text ". About 59% of the surface of the Moon is visible from Earth owing to the different angles at which the Moon can appear in Earth's sky ("
    @.971 link "libration" description="Libration" url="/wiki/Libration"
    @.973 text "), making parts of the "
    @.974 link "far side of the Moon" description="Far side of the Moon" url="/wiki/Far_side_of_the_Moon"
    @.976 text " visible.The Moon has been an important source of inspiration and knowledge in human history, having been crucial to "
    @.978 link "cosmography" description="Cosmography" url="/wiki/Cosmography"
    @.980 text ", "
    @.981 link "mythology" description="Mythology" url="/wiki/Mythology"
    @.983 text ", "
    @.984 link "religion" description="Lunar deity" url="/wiki/Lunar_deity"
    @.986 text ", art, "
    @.987 link "time keeping" description="Lunar calendar" url="/wiki/Lunar_calendar"
    @.989 text ", "
    @.990 link "natural science" description="Natural science" url="/wiki/Natural_science"
    @.992 text " and "
    @.993 link "spaceflight" description="Spaceflight" url="/wiki/Spaceflight"
    @.995 text ". The "
    @.996 link "first spaceflights" description="Timeline of Solar System exploration" url="/wiki/Timeline_of_Solar_System_exploration"
    @.998 text " to an extraterrestrial body were to the Moon, starting in 1959 with the "
    @.999 link "flyby" description="Flyby (spaceflight)" url="/wiki/Flyby_(spaceflight)"
    @.1001 text " of "
    @.1002 link "Luna 1" url="/wiki/Luna_1"
    @.1004 text " (sent by the "
    @.1005 link "Soviet Union" url="/wiki/Soviet_Union"
    @.1007 text "), and the "
    @.1008 link "intentional impact" description="Impactor (spacecraft)" url
... [tee capped at 4096 of 11961 bytes]

